{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1772838593,"nonce":"5wBKsTkfDq8ETNIhaL8JZX2AJZ4=","title":"Dashboard: auth request commit message shows stale COMMIT_MSG.txt content","message":"## Problem\nThe commit message box on auth request cards shows the same stale message every time (\"add gzip compression for conversation blocks content\") — this is leftover from the last time COMMIT_MSG.txt was used. COMMIT_MSG.txt has been eradicated from the workflow (dotfiles issue 8b0b5e2), but the auth-request submission still falls back to reading it from the protected branch worktree.\n\n## Expected Behavior\nFor a merge, the card should show the merge commit message that will be used (e.g., \"Merge branch feature/X into main\"). The field should probably be labeled \"Merge message\" rather than \"Commit message\". If no explicit message is provided via --commit-message, it should show the default git merge message or be left blank rather than showing stale COMMIT_MSG.txt content.\n\n## Root Cause (likely)\nauth-request.sh reads COMMIT_MSG.txt as a fallback when no --commit-message flag is provided. Since the file still exists on the protected branch from historical commits, it finds an ancient one.\n\n## Fix\nRemove COMMIT_MSG.txt reading logic from auth-request.sh. If --commit-message is not provided, either:\n1. Generate a default merge message (\"Merge \u003cbranch\u003e: \u003ctitle\u003e\")\n2. Leave blank and let git use its default\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.6 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","files":null}]}